    "body": "## Summary\n\nAdds **ADR-007: DOM Ownership Model** — the decision that our DOM owns its tree rather than borrowing lexbor's parse tree.\n\nThis is the first of two ADRs needed before any Phase 3 (DOM layer) code. The second (ADR-008: DOM type design in C++) will follow once this lands.\n\n## The Decision\n\n**Own the tree.** After parsing, we walk lexbor's tree once, construct our own nodes (with `unique_ptr` children, `std::string` text, `unordered_map` attributes), and discard lexbor's document.\n\n## Why It Matters\n\nThis decision shapes everything downstream: how nodes are stored, whether we can attach styles and layout boxes to them, and how tightly the renderer is coupled to lexbor.\n\nFour reasons recorded in the ADR:\n\n1. **Long-term architecture.** Every production browser owns its DOM; the parser is a producer.\n2. **Extension fields.** Phase 4+ needs per-node computed styles, layout boxes, paint commands. lexbor's fixed C structs cannot carry these.\n3. **Stable lifetime.** Our DOM outlives lexbor's document.\n4. **Decoupling.** After Phase 3, only `renderer/html` includes lexbor headers.\n\n## Alternatives Considered\n\n- **Borrow from lexbor** — rejected: couples the renderer to lexbor's data layout and prevents per-node extension.\n- **Hybrid: own nodes, borrow text** — rejected: worst of both worlds. Little memory savings, and text `string_view`s would dangle if lexbor's document is destroyed.\n\n## Open Question Deferred\n\nThe ADR notes that lexbor's selectors module operates on lexbor's tree. Once we own our DOM, we'll need our own CSS selector matching (Phase 4). Two options are listed; the recommendation is to implement selectors ourselves, aligned with ADR-006's philosophy of building the interesting parts. A separate ADR will cover that.\n\n## Status\n\nMarked **Accepted**, following the project's ADR convention.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] Follows the ADR format used by ADR-001, ADR-006\n- [x] Rationale and alternatives recorded\n- [x] Consequences documented\n- [x] Open questions deferred to future ADRs\n\n## Related\n\n- ADR-001 (technology stack) — establishes lexbor\n- ADR-006 (return to ADR-001) — mandates building a node tree on top of lexbor\n",
